    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Scientists often rely on the \"herd immunity threshold\" model, specifically the healthcare subsidy effect or public good game theory, to demonstrate that high coverage rates are the only mathematical way to interrupt R0 and stop an epidemic before chaotic containment becomes necessary. On the legal side, civil liberties advocates typically invoke the right to bodily autonomy and the \"right to refuse medical treatment,\" arguing that the state cannot impose one person's biological risk onto another without a compelling individual justification, a principle often rooted in the Hippocratic tradition of patient consent. Furthermore, they frequently critique the \"paternalistic\" aspect of mandates, suggesting that in a free society, the burden of proof for restricting liberty should lie heavily with the state and that any exception (like vaccine hesitancy) must run the other direction.",
